  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 PM EDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nAn expansive upper ridge over the desert southwest with record \nwarmth over parts of the western CONUS.  Downstream troughing to the \neast, placing the Great Lakes in an active west to northwest \nupper flow pattern. As is typical in mid summer patterns, a \nchallenging forecast with weak forcing and somewhat nebulous \ntriggers. This will change by Monday with a stronger shortwave \nand surface frontal passage. \n\nA weak mid level shortwave and washed out surface frontal boundary \nmay bring a few showers or a storm across the area tonight. \nInstability is rather anemic and we remain at least weakly capped \nthrough the first part of tonight.  But there may be enough moisture \npooling along the boundary (evident in narrow 925-850mb theta e \nridge) and forcing along the weakish boundary to produce\nscattered precipitation. Sunday will likely be relatively quiet\nfor much of the day as instability increases under broad warm \nair advection but weak forcing limits storm development.\n\nThat changes by Sunday night and Monday, with much better \nshower and storm chances as a robust shortwave dives across the \nGreat Lakes. Models are coming into better agreement, although \nstill some differences in the strength of this feature and the \nattendant surface reflection, which will certainly have a large \nimpact on the scope of precipitation development (and severity).\nIncreasing mid level westerlies, steepening lapse rates and \nfavorable kinematics within broad low level warm air advection, \nsupport a severe storm threat Sunday night through Monday night,\nwith damaging winds and large hail possible. Many question \nmarks on the overall evolution of the convection, given the \nuncertainty in the strength of the approaching shortwave and the\nplacement of boundaries from earlier storms. The convection may\neventually transition into an MCS as it pushes southward \nthrough Michigan. \n\nWe will likely see some smoke make a return to the area as well over \nthe next few days, although the source region for this smoke is more \nPacific Northwest versus northern Ontario.  Any smoke that returns \ndoesn't look nearly as thick as what was experienced a few weeks \nago. \n\nOtherwise, quieter weather returns through midweek with high \npressure in control.",
